l.\\n\\n5. F3: Only one, so no.\\n\\n6. J1: Already a pair.\\n\\nSo maybe I need to look for possible Chows or Pungs with the existing tiles. Let me check again. \\n\\nLooking at the W3 and W4. If I can get W2 or W5 to complete a Chow. But I don't have those. Alternatively, maybe I can discard one of these and wait for a better tile. \\n\\nWhat about T5 and T6? They could form part of a Chow if I get T4 or T7. But again, not in my hand.\\n\\nB5 is a single tile. Not helpful unless I can get two more. \\n\\nF3 is a single tile. \\n\\nSo maybe the best approach is to look for tiles that can form Pungs or Chows with the existing ones. Let's see:\\n\\n- J1 is a pair. If I can form another Pung or Kong of J1, that would be good. But I have two, so if I draw another J1, that's a Pung. But there are 4 J1 in the game. I have two, so two left. But the unshown tiles show J1: 4. Wait, the unshown tiles list says J1:4, which means there are four J1 remaining. Wait, no. Wait, the unshown tiles are the counts of each tile remaining in the wall. So if my hand has J1:2, then the unshown J1 is 4, meaning there are 4 left in the wall. So total J1 in the game is 4. I have 2, so there are 2 left. So the chance of getting another J1 is possible. But forming a Pung would require three, so if I have two and get one more, that's three. So if I keep J1, maybe I can get another to make a Pung. \\n\\nAlternatively, maybe I can look for other melds. Let's see:\\n\\nLooking at the melds I have, B6-B7-B8. Maybe I can form another Chow in the same suit. For example, B4-B5-B6. I have B5, but need B4 and B6. But I have B6 in the meld. Wait, the meld is B6-B7-B8, so I already have B6. If I have B4 and B5, but I don't have B4. So that's not possible.\\n\\nAlternatively, maybe a Pung of B5? But I have only one.\\n\\nLooking at the other suits. For example, W3 and W4. If I can get W5 to make a Chow W3-W4-W5. But I don't have W5. \\n\\nT5 and T6. If I get T4 or T7 to make a Chow. \\n\\nHmm. Maybe I need